                "annotations": [],
                "text": "Analyse the examples:\nIn each grid, there are 3 panels separated by columns of 3s (green):\nCols are:\n0: 3\n1..4: panel 1 (width 4)\n5: 3\n6..9: panel 2 (width 4)\n10: 3\n11..14: panel 3 (width 4)\n15: 3\n\nLet's check the panels:\nIn Train 1:\nInput:\nPanel 1: empty (0s)\nPanel 2: (0,0) has 4.\nPanel 3: (0,0), (0,1), (1,1) have 4. Wait:\nRow 0: [4, 4, 0, 0]\nRow 1: [0, 4, 0, 0]\nOutput is identical to Input!\n\nWait, Train 2:\nInput:\nPanel 1: (0,0)\nPanel 2: (0,0), (1,1)\nPanel 3: (0,0), (0,1), (1,1), (2,2)\nOutput is identical to Input!\n\nTrain 3:\nInput:\nPanel 1: (0,0), (0,1), (1,1), (2,2)\nPanel 2: (0,0)\nPanel 3: (0,0), (1,1)\nOutput:\nPanel 1: (0,0)\nPanel 2: (0,0), (1,1)\nPanel 3: (0,0), (0,1), (1,1), (2,2)\n\nLook at that! In Train 3, the panels are sorted!\nBy what?\nNumber of yellow (4) pixels?\nIn Train 1:\nPanel 1 has 0 pixels.\nPanel 2 has 1 pixel.\nPanel 3 has 3 pixels.\nCounts: 0, 1, 3 (already sorted in ascending order!).\n\nIn Train 2:\nPanel 1 has 1 pixel.\nPanel 2 has 2 pixels.\nPanel 3 has 4 pixels.\nCounts: 1, 2, 4 (already sorted!).\n\nIn Train 3:\nPanel 1 has 4 pixels.\nPanel 2 has 1 pixel.\nPanel 3 has 2 pixels.\nCounts: 4, 1, 2.\nOutput: sorted order: 1 (Panel 2), 2 (Panel 3), 4 (Panel 1).\nWait! In Train 3:\nOutput Panel 1: [4,0,0,0], [0,0,0,0]... that was Panel 2 (count 1)!\nOutput Panel 2: [4,0,0,0], [0,4,0,0]... that was Panel 3 (count 2)!\nOutput Panel 3: [4,4,0,0], [0,4,0,0], [0,0,4,0]... that was Panel 1 (count 4)!\n\nThe panels are sorted by the number of yellow (4) pixels in ascending order!Ready for the held-out test inputs.",
